---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-b2`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin B2 (Riboflavin) Reference',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamins & Minerals', item: `${SITE}/vitamins` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Vitamin B2', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin B2 (Riboflavin): Migraine Prophylaxis and Bright Yellow Urine"
  description="Riboflavin reference — RDA, migraine prevention evidence at 400 mg/day, MTHFR interaction, and why B-complex supplements turn urine fluorescent yellow."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamins", label: "Vitamins & Minerals" }, { href: "/vitamin-b2", label: "Vitamin B2" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin B2 (Riboflavin)</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Riboflavin is the precursor to FAD and FMN, redox cofactors central to mitochondrial
      energy production. Deficiency alone is rare in high-income countries but common as
      part of general B-complex insufficiency in alcoholism and severe undernutrition.
      Riboflavin has one well-established therapeutic use — migraine prevention.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Daily requirements</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Adult men:</strong> 1.3 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Adult women:</strong> 1.1 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 1.4 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Lactation:</strong> 1.6 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>No Tolerable Upper Intake Level</strong> — excess is renally excreted (as riboflavin's bright yellow pigment).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Migraine prevention</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Riboflavin 400 mg/day has Level B evidence from the American Academy of Neurology
      for migraine prophylaxis. The seminal 1998 Schoenen trial showed a 50% reduction in
      migraine frequency in about half of participants after 3 months. Response is slow
      (allow 2–3 months). It's cheap, safe, and worth trying before or alongside prescription
      preventives like topiramate or CGRP antagonists.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Ariboflavinosis (deficiency)</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Cheilosis (cracks at corners of the mouth), angular stomatitis, glossitis, seborrhoeic
      dermatitis, corneal vascularisation, and normocytic-normochromic anaemia. Usually
      responds within days to riboflavin 5–10 mg/day. Common in alcoholism, anorexia, and
      breast-fed infants of B2-deficient mothers.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">MTHFR polymorphism</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Riboflavin is a cofactor for methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase (MTHFR). People
      homozygous for the C677T polymorphism (~10% of Europeans) have reduced enzyme activity
      that is partly restored by higher riboflavin intake. Small trials show riboflavin
      lowers blood pressure specifically in this genotype — an interesting example of
      nutrigenomics with clinical relevance.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The fluorescent yellow urine</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Riboflavin itself is a bright yellow-green fluorescent pigment. Any dose above a few
      mg (which includes almost every multivitamin and B-complex) produces neon-yellow
      urine within hours — harmless, and actually confirms your supplement is absorbing.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Food sources</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Beef liver (3 oz): 2.9 mg — the champion source</li>
      <li>Fortified breakfast cereal: 0.6–2 mg</li>
      <li>Yoghurt, plain (1 cup): 0.5 mg</li>
      <li>Milk (1 cup): 0.4 mg</li>
      <li>Egg (1 large): 0.2 mg</li>
      <li>Almonds (1 oz): 0.3 mg</li>
      <li>Beef (3 oz): 0.4 mg</li>
      <li>Mushrooms, portabella (½ cup): 0.3 mg</li>
      <li>Spinach (½ cup cooked): 0.2 mg</li>
    </ul>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> If you're using 400 mg/day riboflavin for
      migraine and starting a new headache pattern (worse pain, aura, unilateral neurology),
      get medical review — riboflavin is prophylaxis, not treatment for a red-flag headache.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-b2" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
